-**Parameters:**
-- $\epsilon$: Typical value $10^{-4}$ to $10^{-6}$ for visual quality.
-- Max iterations: 100 is sufficient for most convergent methods.
+**Parameters**
+*   **Tolerance (`tol`):** Convergence threshold for $|f_t(t)|$. Typical value: $10^{-6}$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Max Iterations (`max_iter`):** Limit for iterative solvers. Typical value: 100 [S1, unofficial].
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Multiple Roots:** Simple Newton-Raphson may converge to a local root or diverge. Robust methods (bracketing, interval arithmetic) are required for general forms [S1, unofficial].
-- **Non-monotonic Behavior:** If $f_t(t)$ is not monotonic, sign changes may not indicate a single root. Interval arithmetic or Lipschitz bounds help isolate roots [S1, unofficial].
-- **Derivative Zero:** Newton-Raphson fails if $f_t'(t) \approx 0$. Fallback to bisection is necessary [G].
-- **Precision:** Floating-point errors can cause false positives/negatives in root isolation. Interval arithmetic mitigates this [S1, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Multiple Roots:** Simple closed-form or Newton-Raphson may fail for complex implicit functions with multiple roots or nonmonotonic behavior along the ray interval [S1, unofficial]. Robust techniques like Sturm sequences or interval arithmetic are required [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Non-Differentiable Functions:** Newton-Raphson requires derivatives. If $f$ is not differentiable, use bracketing methods or finite differences [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Acceleration Structure Failure:** If the acceleration structure fails to cull empty space, performance degrades to $O(N)$ [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Isosurface Ray Tracing
-Renders $f(\mathbf{x}) - v = 0$. Sets apart by allowing dynamic isovalue changes without re-meshing. Choose when isovalue varies or is user-defined. [Isosurface Ray Tracing](52-isosurface-ray-tracing.md)
+### Kalra & Barr Implicit Surface Intersection
+Uses Lipschitz bounds for robust intersection of algebraic and non-algebraic surfaces. Choose when robustness for complex algebraic surfaces is needed. [Kalra & Barr Implicit Surface Intersection](1278-kalra-barr-implicit-surface-intersection.md)
 
-### Ray Tracing General-Form Implicits
-Handles arbitrary $f(\mathbf{x}) = 0$, including non-algebraic functions. Sets apart by using interval/affine arithmetic for robustness. Choose for complex procedural implicits. [Ray Tracing General-Form Implicits](53-ray-tracing-general-form-implicits.md)
+### SDF Raycast Collision Detection
+Uses Signed Distance Functions for efficient ray marching. Choose when distance bounds are available and the surface is smooth. [SDF Raycast Collision Detection](2417-sdf-raycast-collision-detection.md)
 
-### GPU Ray Casting of Implicits
-Uses fragment shaders to cast rays through bounding proxies. Sets apart by leveraging GPU parallelism for primary rays. Choose for interactive applications with limited secondary rays. [GPU Ray Casting of Implicits](54-gpu-ray-casting-of-implicits.md)
+### LevelSetHDDA
+Hybrid Depth-First/Depth-First approach for level set rendering. Choose for volume data with hierarchical structures. [LevelSetHDDA](2875-levelsethdda.md)
+
+### Ray-Ellipsoid Intersection
+Closed-form solution for ellipsoids. Choose for simple quadratic primitives. [Ray-Ellipsoid Intersection](3463-ray-ellipsoid-intersection.md)
+
+### RayImplicitIntersection
+General intersection routine. Choose for generic implicit primitives. [RayImplicitIntersection](3591-rayimplicitintersection.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(\log N)$ per pixel with acceleration structures (e.g., hierarchical grids, BVHs), where $N$ is the number of geometric primitives or voxels. Without acceleration, $O(N)$ [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the acceleration structure. Hierarchical grids for volumes can be large but allow efficient traversal.
-- **Measured Performance:** Interactive rendering of large (1 GB) structured volumes achieved on 128-CPU workstations using tile-based parallel ray tracing [S1, unofficial]. GPU implementations enable interactive ray casting of cubics and quartics [S1, unofficial].
-- **Scaling:** Scales well with parallelization (CPU/GPU). Acceleration structures reduce cost significantly for complex scenes.
-- **Strengths:** No mesh extraction overhead; correct rendering at any resolution/camera position; supports dynamic isovalue; handles complex geometry naturally.
-- **Limitations:** Slower than rasterization of simple proxy geometry; requires robust numerical methods for general forms; acceleration structure construction can be costly for dynamic data.
-- **When to Use:** Visualization of large structured data (e.g., medical volumes); dynamic isosurface exploration; complex procedural implicits where mesh extraction is impractical.
-- **When Not to Use:** Simple static geometry where mesh extraction is cheap; real-time applications with strict latency constraints and simple scenes.
+*   **Time Complexity:** With acceleration structures, each pixel is rendered at roughly $O(\log N)$ cost with respect to the number of geometric primitives. Without acceleration, or for rasterization, the cost is $O(N)$ for the entire frame [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the acceleration structure and the storage of the implicit function data (e.g., voxel grid, polynomial coefficients). Not given in the sources.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Interactive rendering of isosurfaces from large (1 GB) structured volumes has been achieved on 128-CPU workstations using hierarchical grids [S1, unofficial]. GPU implementations have demonstrated interactive ray casting of cubics and quartics [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Parallelization on GPUs or multi-core CPUs allows efficient rendering of large scenes. Scaling is limited by memory bandwidth for large voxel grids or the complexity of the implicit function evaluation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** No mesh extraction required; correct rendering regardless of camera position; on-the-fly changes in isovalue; native support for secondary rays; avoids Nyquist limit issues of proxy geometry [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Root-solving is not as fast as rasterization of simpler proxy geometry; robust methods can be computationally expensive; requires efficient acceleration structures for complex scenes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Isosurface visualization of large structured data; dynamic isovalue changes; scenes with complex implicit geometry where mesh extraction is prohibitive [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Simple scenes where rasterization of proxy geometry is faster; real-time applications with strict performance constraints and simple primitives [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Ray tracing implicit surfaces was first explored by Hanrahan [39] using Descartes' rule of signs. Van Wijk [122] implemented a recursive root bracketing algorithm using Sturm sequences, employed in POV-Ray [96]. Mitchell [82] suggested interval arithmetic for root isolation. De Cusatis Junior et al. [16] extended this to affine arithmetic. Loop and Blinn [72] implemented a GPU ray caster using Bernstein polynomials. Fryazinov and Pasko [27] used rule-of-signs interval methods for FRep surfaces on GPU. Parker et al. [90, 89] achieved interactive rendering of large structured volumes using hierarchical grids.
